1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,800
Caesar altered his alphabet.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:04,800
The Nazis had enigma.

3
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:07,300
Computers and digital encryption.

4
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:11,600
The attempts to make and break these secret methods of scrambled communication

5
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:13,800
have changed the course of history.

6
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,600
Now, Codes on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,580
Manila, the Philippines, January 6, 1995.

8
00:00:34,580 --> 00:00:37,360
When police responded to an apartment fire,

9
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:42,480
they found a Toshiba laptop amid a batch of chemicals and bomb-making materials.

10
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:48,620
An open file on the laptop revealed a plan to simultaneously bomb 12 U.S.

11
00:00:48,621 --> 00:00:50,440
airliners over the Pacific.

12
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:54,640
The potential death toll, 4,000 passengers.

13
00:00:54,641 --> 00:01:01,340
several other files used encryption software that scrambled the writings in secret code

14
00:01:01,341 --> 00:01:06,840
the Philippine police decoded some of the files using a special computer program

15
00:01:06,841 --> 00:01:10,280
and uncovered a list of bomb making recipes

16
00:01:10,281 --> 00:01:15,040
all the evidence pointed to the most hunted terrorist in the world

17
00:01:15,041 --> 00:01:17,260
Ramzi Yousef

18
00:01:17,261 --> 00:01:20,400
wanted for his involvement in the World Trade Center bombing

19
00:01:20,401 --> 00:01:27,400
Yousef's plots were stopped and he was in US custody within six weeks.

20
00:01:27,401 --> 00:01:33,400
It was a major victory for international law enforcement and revealed the importance of cryptology,

21
00:01:33,401 --> 00:01:37,400
the science of making and breaking secret codes.

22
00:01:37,401 --> 00:01:42,400
To trace the development of codes is to uncover the very fabric of civilization.

23
00:01:42,401 --> 00:01:48,400
Once a culture reaches a level of sophistication in literacy, science and language,

24
00:01:48,401 --> 00:01:51,740
the demand for secret symbolic communication flourishes.

25
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:57,580
I think codes go back probably as far as there is language.

26
00:01:58,300 --> 00:01:59,680
People had something to keep secret.

27
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:01,880
They needed to find some way to scramble it.

28
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:06,920
For centuries, codes have been controlled by governments

29
00:02:06,921 --> 00:02:12,260
employed in war, applied in diplomacy, and used in espionage.

30
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:15,140
But with modern technology,

31
00:02:15,141 --> 00:02:21,620
the use of codes by individuals has exploded, enriching cryptology and empowering citizens.

32
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:28,460
A communications boom has heightened the need for codes to keep the information private on the Internet,

33
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:33,280
at ATMs, and in medical offices.

34
00:02:36,260 --> 00:02:40,260
Today's complex codes are the result of a historic seesaw battle

35
00:02:40,261 --> 00:02:43,000
between code makers and code breakers

36
00:02:43,001 --> 00:02:45,860
that has pushed the very boundaries of science.

37
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:50,980
The earliest known code was an inscription carved into a rock

38
00:02:50,981 --> 00:02:53,860
by an Egyptian scribe in 1900 BC.

39
00:02:54,980 --> 00:02:59,140
It was an attempt to demonstrate wider knowledge by dressing up the writing.

40
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:03,900
Ordinary hieroglyphics were replaced with unusual symbols

41
00:03:03,901 --> 00:03:07,580
which told the story of the writer's master, K'numhotep II.

42
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:13,333
Another early code was used for secrecy.

43
00:03:13,334 --> 00:03:23,550
A tiny enciphered tablet found on the banks of the Tigris River, dating from 1500 BC, contained a hidden formula for glazing pottery.

44
00:03:23,551 --> 00:03:28,620
And the ancient Greeks created their own inventive coding methods.

45
00:03:28,621 --> 00:03:38,060
One of the subordinates of a Persian monarch thought that it might be time to revolt.

46
00:03:38,061 --> 00:03:47,740
And he had an agent in the court of Darius the Great, and this agent sent a message to this man saying it's now time to revolt.

47
00:03:47,741 --> 00:03:50,040
he shaved the head of one of his slaves

48
00:03:50,041 --> 00:03:54,000
and tattooed on the head the word revolt

49
00:03:54,001 --> 00:03:56,300
and waited for the hair to grow

50
00:03:56,301 --> 00:03:59,700
and sent the slave down to this man's palace.

51
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:02,620
When the man got the slave, he shaved the head,

52
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,980
saw the word revolt and realized that this was the time

53
00:04:05,981 --> 00:04:08,020
and he did revolt successfully.

54
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:13,160
In Sparta, a new instrument to encipher messages

55
00:04:13,161 --> 00:04:14,560
between legions was used.

56
00:04:14,561 --> 00:04:16,580
It was called a skittaly.

57
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:21,940
A cloth was wrapped around a stick and a message written down the side.

58
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:24,900
Wrapped around the stick, the message could be read.

59
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:26,720
Unwrapped, it was scrambled.

60
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:29,460
The message was sent from general to general.

61
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:33,060
Unwrapped, the generals would have a stick of the appropriate length,

62
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:35,400
could wrap the cloth around it and read the message.

63
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:37,960
Every code had a key,

64
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:43,000
a secret method of scrambling and unscrambling messages used by sender and receiver.

65
00:04:43,001 --> 00:04:50,840
Julius Caesar enciphered his military communications using a key that employed an alternative alphabet

66
00:04:50,841 --> 00:04:54,200
by moving each letter three spaces to the right.

67
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,200
But with the collapse of the Roman Empire in 500 A.D.,

68
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,920
European cryptology entered a dark age that would last for a thousand years.

69
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:10,100
But another civilization was rising in the east.

70
00:05:10,101 --> 00:05:15,980
by 900 AD Arab society was one of the most literate cultures in the world and

71
00:05:15,981 --> 00:05:22,760
the study of codes flourished the Arabs were the first to record the methods of

72
00:05:22,761 --> 00:05:28,400
transposition the scrambling of letters as well as substitution the replacement

73
00:05:28,401 --> 00:05:33,500
of letters with numbers and symbols they were the first to outline specific

74
00:05:33,501 --> 00:05:39,920
techniques of code breaking that involved mathematical or frequency analysis

75
00:05:39,921 --> 00:05:44,900
The Arabs were very interested in letter studies,

76
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:50,420
and they counted the number of times a character such as Aleph appeared in the Koran,

77
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:53,460
and they discovered which letters began words frequently.

78
00:05:54,060 --> 00:06:00,960
They used this information, a statistical source, to learn how to break codes.

79
00:06:00,961 --> 00:06:05,200
The most frequent coded letter represents the most frequent plain text letter,

80
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:09,180
and that's how you begin to solve a code, and they were the first to do this.

81
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:14,120
With the Renaissance, cryptology had a rebirth in Europe.

82
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:19,100
In 1466, an Italian architect, Leon Alberti,

83
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:22,780
developed the greatest cryptologic invention in a thousand years

84
00:06:22,781 --> 00:06:24,200
at the urging of the Vatican.

85
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:28,680
It was a system of rotating cipher disks

86
00:06:28,681 --> 00:06:31,880
with two rings of letters and several numbers.

87
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:34,800
By scrambling so many letters randomly,

88
00:06:34,801 --> 00:06:39,180
it was the first to challenge the Arab code-breaking method of frequency analysis.

89
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:45,520
Previous systems just replaced A with D only one at a time.

90
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:54,560
The Alberti cipher disk used a disk in which these letter combinations could be changed from time to time.

91
00:06:54,561 --> 00:07:00,140
And as a consequence, A would be represented not by only one letter, but by many letters.

92
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:06,060
This was called polyalphabetic, many alphabet substitution.

93
00:07:06,061 --> 00:07:10,020
and it was the basis for many modern cipher systems.

94
00:07:11,180 --> 00:07:16,800
A French diplomat, Blaise de Visionnaire, improved the Alberti system in 1586

95
00:07:16,801 --> 00:07:21,000
by creating a grid of 26 cipher alphabets.

96
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:24,080
Each shifted one over from the last.

97
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:31,340
By use of a key word, encoding and decoding communications using Visionnaire's square

98
00:07:31,341 --> 00:07:36,260
could be calculated on a sheet of paper instead of having to carry a disc.

99
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:41,540
Unfortunately, Mary, Queen of Scots, did not employ this new development.

100
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:47,820
Beginning in 1568, Mary was imprisoned by her cousin, Queen Elizabeth I of England.

101
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:53,480
In 1586, a group of Catholics sought to rescue Mary

102
00:07:53,481 --> 00:07:56,540
and put her on England's throne after assassinating Elizabeth.

103
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:01,440
Her rescuers communicated with Mary through secret coded letters.

104
00:08:02,460 --> 00:08:07,440
Her great mistake was in thinking that her cipher system was secure.

105
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:14,140
It was actually being read by Queen Elizabeth's code breakers and everything about the plot was known.

106
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:21,720
And when it reached a certain critical point, Queen Elizabeth called a halt to it and sentenced Mary, Queen of Scots, to death.

107
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:27,420
By the 1700s, each European power had its own black chamber,

108
00:08:28,020 --> 00:08:31,060
a nerve center for deciphering messages and gathering intelligence.

109
00:08:31,740 --> 00:08:35,220
But the real revolution in cryptology was just ahead.

110
00:08:36,580 --> 00:08:43,380
Telegraph, for the first time, made it possible to send messages quickly over long distances.

111
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:48,520
In fact, it made the United States a continental power by increasing its communications power.

112
00:08:48,521 --> 00:08:52,940
It also raised the necessity for cryptology.

113
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:58,600
In 1844, Samuel Morse invented Morse code,

114
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:02,980
a series of electrical dots and dashes to communicate over the telegraph.

115
00:09:03,980 --> 00:09:07,720
Because the code was public and messages could be easily intercepted,

116
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:10,840
the need for new secret codes became acute.

117
00:09:11,740 --> 00:09:14,420
But the telegraph paled in comparison

118
00:09:14,421 --> 00:09:18,220
to the communication industry's next scientific development,

119
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:24,840
one that revolutionized codes and was used with murderous impact in the 20th century.

120
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:31,320
Secret flag signals were used extensively on the battlefield in the American Civil War.

121
00:09:33,080 --> 00:09:35,600
Codes will return on Modern Marvels.

122
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:42,560
At the dawn of the 20th century, an Italian physicist, Guglielmo Marconi,

123
00:09:43,060 --> 00:09:44,860
changed the world of codes forever.

124
00:09:44,861 --> 00:09:50,400
Marconi's invention harnessed radio waves as a new method of communication.

125
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:56,500
The result was an instantaneous transmission of messages across long distances.

126
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:01,740
The invention of the radio was the most important single event in the history of codes and ciphers.

127
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:07,060
Radio had the great effect of facilitating communications.

128
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:09,780
You don't have to lay wire or anything like that.

129
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:11,780
You can just go out with a radio post and communicate.

130
00:10:11,781 --> 00:10:19,160
but it has the great disadvantage of turning over to the enemy every message that you send.

131
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:24,840
So this means you have to put into code every message that you send,

132
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,360
so it stimulated enormously the great growth of codes and ciphers.

133
00:10:30,920 --> 00:10:34,820
The ramifications of this new invention were not lost upon the generals.

134
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:39,640
Radio allowed quick communication with field divisions

135
00:10:39,641 --> 00:10:42,400
and a more rapid mobile form of warfare.

136
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:48,400
Radio messages were enciphered and sent in Morse code across the airwaves.

137
00:10:49,720 --> 00:10:53,660
But radio transmission revealed every cryptogram it conveyed.

138
00:10:54,660 --> 00:10:58,600
It furnished a constant and massive stream of intercepts to the enemy.

139
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:01,720
With the outbreak of World War I,

140
00:11:01,721 --> 00:11:05,620
the need for new codes and code breakers sharply escalated.

141
00:11:05,621 --> 00:11:09,940
A global battle of secret communications had begun.

142
00:11:11,140 --> 00:11:19,300
On August 5, 1914, British divers from the ship Talconia severed German cable lines in the North Atlantic.

143
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:24,760
It appears their idea was simply to make things more difficult for the Germans,

144
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:31,940
but it had the payoff of forcing a great deal of traffic that would have gone by cable onto radio.

145
00:11:31,941 --> 00:11:39,640
And so it resulted in really a flood of intercepts coming in that otherwise wouldn't have been available.

146
00:11:40,280 --> 00:11:46,300
Once intercepted, messages were sent to Room 40, the cryptanalysis section of the British Admiralty.

147
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:51,580
Room 40 might be the prototype of modern cryptanalytic organizations.

148
00:11:52,180 --> 00:11:58,900
They recruited mathematicians, linguists, chess masters, anybody who was good at puzzle solving.

149
00:11:59,840 --> 00:12:05,960
In September 1914, Britain received one of the greatest gifts in the history of cryptology.

150
00:12:07,320 --> 00:12:12,280
Her allies, the Russians, captured the German cruiser Magdeburg in the Baltic

151
00:12:12,281 --> 00:12:15,000
and a book of Germany's main naval code.

152
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:19,140
They promptly turned the codebook over to Room 40,

153
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:22,200
allowing Britain to break German naval messages

154
00:12:22,201 --> 00:12:25,560
and bottle up Germany's battleships for the duration of the war.

155
00:12:25,561 --> 00:12:29,800
code breaking had transformed modern combat

156
00:12:29,801 --> 00:12:34,420
being able to read the German naval signals

157
00:12:34,421 --> 00:12:37,940
often almost instantaneously

158
00:12:37,941 --> 00:12:43,440
was something that simply hadn't happened before in warfare

159
00:12:43,441 --> 00:12:46,300
on the eastern front

160
00:12:46,301 --> 00:12:48,500
cryptography or lack of it

161
00:12:48,501 --> 00:12:51,460
led to one of the most critical battles in World War I

162
00:12:51,461 --> 00:12:54,880
in late August 1914

163
00:12:54,881 --> 00:12:58,220
the Russians sent two armies into East Prussia

164
00:12:58,221 --> 00:13:02,140
in a pinchers movement to trap the Germans near the village of Tannenburg.

165
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,960
The Germans were outmanned,

166
00:13:05,540 --> 00:13:09,000
but had the advantage of listening in on Russian radio communications.

167
00:13:10,260 --> 00:13:13,740
Due to the Russians' rudimentary and disorganized coding system,

168
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:17,400
intercepts told the Germans that the Russian southern army

169
00:13:17,401 --> 00:13:19,820
was moving faster than its northern force.

170
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:24,060
The Germans wheeled and destroyed the Russian southern army,

171
00:13:24,061 --> 00:13:29,300
capturing 100,000 prisoners with an estimated 30,000 dead or missing.

172
00:13:31,140 --> 00:13:35,320
This was the first great defeat of the Russians in the war

173
00:13:35,321 --> 00:13:40,240
and it started them on their long slide into ruin and revolution.

174
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:46,180
The Russians did not at World War I have a good cipher system

175
00:13:46,181 --> 00:13:48,500
and in part they lost the war because of it.

176
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:52,680
But the greatest code-breaking event in the history of cryptology

177
00:13:52,681 --> 00:13:55,660
began on January 17, 1917.

178
00:13:56,880 --> 00:13:59,100
The British intercepted a telegram

179
00:13:59,101 --> 00:14:02,020
that was encrypted with the highest German diplomatic code,

180
00:14:02,780 --> 00:14:03,960
0075.

181
00:14:05,540 --> 00:14:09,520
It was a mind-numbing system of 10,000 words and phrases

182
00:14:09,521 --> 00:14:12,380
attached to a thousand numerical code groups.

183
00:14:13,640 --> 00:14:16,900
The secret message was from the German foreign minister,

184
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:18,080
Arthur Zimmerman,

185
00:14:18,840 --> 00:14:20,380
to his ambassador in Washington,

186
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:22,380
Johann von Bernstorff,

187
00:14:22,680 --> 00:14:27,840
for relay to Germany's ambassador to Mexico, Heinrich von Eckart.

188
00:14:28,480 --> 00:14:34,560
The telegram would be deciphered there and sent to Mexico's president, Benustiano Carranza.

189
00:14:35,380 --> 00:14:44,740
It was sent from Berlin to Washington, encrypted on the American undersea telegraph cable.

190
00:14:45,260 --> 00:14:48,900
The British intercepted it there, recognized its importance.

191
00:14:48,901 --> 00:14:53,000
It was in a new diplomatic system which the British had not yet broken.

192
00:14:54,260 --> 00:14:58,140
After receiving the message in the new 0075 code,

193
00:14:58,820 --> 00:15:02,280
von Bernstorff's office in Washington then made a fatal mistake.

194
00:15:04,420 --> 00:15:09,220
In Washington, they took this telegram out of the new diplomatic codebook,

195
00:15:10,060 --> 00:15:12,800
re-encrypted it in the old diplomatic codebook,

196
00:15:12,900 --> 00:15:15,540
and sent it to Mexico City on the telegraph.

197
00:15:15,541 --> 00:15:19,540
But the British had already broken the old code.

198
00:15:19,541 --> 00:15:25,540
The British intercepted that version as well, had versions to compare.

199
00:15:25,541 --> 00:15:29,540
Perhaps the silliest, most tragic error that a cryptographer can make.

200
00:15:29,541 --> 00:15:34,540
British cryptanalysts began to decipher the message in the old code.

201
00:15:34,541 --> 00:15:36,540
In painstaking chunks.

202
00:15:36,541 --> 00:15:40,540
Constructing patterns using pencil and paper.

203
00:15:40,541 --> 00:15:42,540
Within days, they had it.

204
00:15:42,541 --> 00:15:43,241
added.

205
00:15:43,242 --> 00:15:48,860
As the meaning of Zimmerman's secret message began to emerge, the import was stunning.

206
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:54,740
Despite the sinking of the American ocean liner Lusitania by the Germans in 1915,

207
00:15:55,760 --> 00:16:00,440
America maintained its neutrality with the promise of German restrictions on its U-boats.

208
00:16:01,380 --> 00:16:07,560
The Zimmerman telegram outlined Germany's intent to resume unrestricted submarine warfare

209
00:16:07,561 --> 00:16:12,640
on February 1, 1917, as a means to strangle Britain.

210
00:16:14,060 --> 00:16:19,900
To keep America at bay, Zimmerman proposed that Mexico invade the United States

211
00:16:19,901 --> 00:16:23,200
and reclaim Texas, New Mexico, and Arizona.

212
00:16:24,680 --> 00:16:28,940
Germany also asked Mexico to persuade Japan to attack America.

213
00:16:29,900 --> 00:16:33,280
The actions would be backed with German military and financial aid.

214
00:16:33,281 --> 00:16:38,780
the British Admiralty was desperate to get the deciphered message to the Americans

215
00:16:38,781 --> 00:16:41,840
without alerting the Germans that their code had been broken

216
00:16:41,841 --> 00:16:47,100
a British agent was able to infiltrate the Mexican telegraph office

217
00:16:47,101 --> 00:16:50,960
and retrieve a deciphered copy of the message to Mexico's president

218
00:16:50,961 --> 00:16:54,740
once they had their cover of a supposed leak in Mexico

219
00:16:54,741 --> 00:16:58,840
the British approached the Americans and revealed the telegram

220
00:16:58,841 --> 00:17:01,740
the whole country blew up

221
00:17:01,741 --> 00:17:03,700
Everybody was incensed.

222
00:17:04,360 --> 00:17:07,380
Formerly, only the East Coast was concerned.

223
00:17:07,820 --> 00:17:11,820
Now, suddenly, the whole Midwest was worried about Mexico.

224
00:17:12,460 --> 00:17:17,780
And six weeks after this message was deciphered, the United States declared war on Germany.

225
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:23,260
So it was probably the most significant event in code-breaking history.

226
00:17:23,261 --> 00:17:31,060
What he did was convince the entire country that Germany was a national enemy

227
00:17:31,061 --> 00:17:35,360
And a few months later, when Woodrow Wilson asked for a declaration of war,

228
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:40,200
he had a united and angry country behind him, ready to go to war against the Germans.

229
00:17:41,180 --> 00:17:48,920
A single breakthrough by the cryptanalysts in Room 40 had succeeded where three years of intensive diplomacy had failed.

230
00:17:50,500 --> 00:17:56,220
Code breaking would play an even greater role in cryptology's golden age, World War II.

231
00:17:56,221 --> 00:18:03,660
At the end of World War I, the power of the code breakers over the code makers seemed obvious.

232
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:11,160
But by 1917, American inventor Edward Hebern conceived the first model of an electrical machine,

233
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:15,160
made with a wired rotor of letters attached to a keyboard.

234
00:18:16,540 --> 00:18:21,020
One year later, a German electrical engineer, Arthur Sherbius,

235
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:25,840
independently created an electrical encryption machine similar to Hebern's.

236
00:18:26,220 --> 00:18:27,736
with several rotors.

237
00:18:27,737 --> 00:18:33,420
The inventions revolutionized cryptography by mechanizing the production of

238
00:18:33,421 --> 00:18:37,296
codes with millions of potential variations.

239
00:18:37,297 --> 00:18:41,260
Imagine a hockey puck, 26 electrical contacts

240
00:18:41,261 --> 00:18:46,759
on one side, 26 electrical contacts on the other side, wired at random.

241
00:18:46,760 --> 00:18:48,540
You shoot an electrical

242
00:18:48,541 --> 00:18:54,472
current through into A. It comes out at Q, lights up a letter.

243
00:18:54,473 --> 00:18:56,960
Now this hockey puck turns

244
00:18:56,961 --> 00:19:03,744
one space. You shoot a current in again at A. Now it's going to come out at X.

245
00:19:03,745 --> 00:19:04,445
And this

246
00:19:04,446 --> 00:19:08,819
continues to turn until you go through all 26 revolutions.

247
00:19:08,820 --> 00:19:11,160
And then instead of just having

248
00:19:11,161 --> 00:19:17,040
one cipher machine, one cipher wheel next to another, you have several rotors next to

249
00:19:17,041 --> 00:19:25,651
another. By the mid-1920s, Sherbius was mass-producing his new model called Enigma.

250
00:19:25,652 --> 00:19:27,000
Over the next

251
00:19:27,001 --> 00:19:32,100
two decades, he would sell more than 30,000 units to the German military that viewed it

252
00:19:32,101 --> 00:19:33,218
as a godsend.

253
00:19:33,219 --> 00:19:39,840
Meanwhile, the cryptology offices of the Allies had dwindled in personnel and

254
00:19:39,841 --> 00:19:40,541
quality.

255
00:19:40,542 --> 00:19:45,080
But Poland, because of its geographical vulnerability between Germany and Russia,

256
00:19:45,081 --> 00:19:46,920
was desperate for intelligence.

257
00:19:48,400 --> 00:19:50,940
The Poles set up a world-leading cryptanalyst bureau

258
00:19:50,941 --> 00:19:54,000
and hired a group of brilliant mathematicians.

259
00:19:54,580 --> 00:19:57,780
One of them was 23-year-old Marian Rajevsky.

260
00:19:58,760 --> 00:20:03,400
What Rajevsky realized was that if you have a machine that's based on rotors,

261
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:05,820
there's certain mathematical relationships

262
00:20:05,821 --> 00:20:10,200
between the way it encyphers a letter in one position

263
00:20:10,201 --> 00:20:14,120
and the way it encyphers a letter in the next position in sequence.

264
00:20:14,121 --> 00:20:18,220
Even today, it's an astonishing accomplishment of what he did,

265
00:20:18,760 --> 00:20:25,320
was able to reconstruct the wiring of the rotors of an Enigma machine without ever having seen one.

266
00:20:26,400 --> 00:20:29,740
In building his own model of the German military Enigma,

267
00:20:30,580 --> 00:20:36,120
all Raevsky had was a commercial version of Enigma and his extraordinary math skills.

268
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:41,920
At the time, only a few hundred German military Enigma units existed,

269
00:20:41,921 --> 00:20:45,320
and all were kept under tight control by the German army.

270
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:51,140
Then in 1931, a German trader, Hans Thilo Schmidt,

271
00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:54,680
supplied background information for using the Enigma machine

272
00:20:54,681 --> 00:20:56,440
which aided Rayefsky's guesswork.

273
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:01,220
The one piece of the puzzle he did not have

274
00:21:01,221 --> 00:21:04,820
was knowing how the keyboard of the Enigma machine

275
00:21:04,821 --> 00:21:07,200
was wired up to the first rotor.

276
00:21:07,540 --> 00:21:11,040
Maybe he had a good sense of the lack of imagination of the Germans

277
00:21:11,041 --> 00:21:15,340
but he thought, well, maybe they wired A to A and B to B and C to C,

278
00:21:15,420 --> 00:21:16,340
and sure enough, that was it.

279
00:21:16,660 --> 00:21:20,020
He tried that, and he said the solution started coming out

280
00:21:20,021 --> 00:21:23,580
as if by magic, almost, on this paper in front of him.

281
00:21:25,280 --> 00:21:28,320
But the Germans routinely changed the daily key,

282
00:21:29,220 --> 00:21:32,760
the indicator setting that determined the scrambling of their daily messages.

283
00:21:35,080 --> 00:21:38,220
The daily key was distributed in a monthly codebook

284
00:21:38,221 --> 00:21:41,980
to both sender and receiver to code and decode messages.

285
00:21:42,740 --> 00:21:44,580
To find the daily key,

286
00:21:45,100 --> 00:21:49,000
Rajevsky built and connected replicas of six Enigma machines together

287
00:21:49,001 --> 00:21:52,900
that ran through more than 17,000 indicator settings.

288
00:21:53,820 --> 00:21:56,820
He called his new contraption the bomb

289
00:21:56,821 --> 00:22:00,060
because of its ticking noise while running the permutations.

290
00:22:01,080 --> 00:22:05,060
Code making had been mechanized with the cipher machines, the Enigma,

291
00:22:05,061 --> 00:22:10,180
And now what the Poles were doing after mathematizing their code breaking

292
00:22:10,181 --> 00:22:17,280
was carrying it the next step further and mechanizing the mathematized code breaking.

293
00:22:18,160 --> 00:22:22,320
It was a great insight when they invented it, a machine to solve a machine.

294
00:22:23,440 --> 00:22:27,140
The Poles secretly read the Enigma traffic for several years

295
00:22:27,141 --> 00:22:30,580
until they hit a huge obstacle in December 1938

296
00:22:30,581 --> 00:22:34,000
when the Germans added two new rotors into the rotation.

297
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:40,840
This exponentially increased the need for more bombs and cryptanalysts to run through the daily settings.

298
00:22:42,040 --> 00:22:47,060
Fearing a German invasion, the Poles finally called in their allies, Britain and France.

299
00:22:47,720 --> 00:22:54,060
In a small brick hut in the Pyrrha Forest south of Warsaw, the Poles revealed their astounding discoveries.

300
00:22:56,920 --> 00:23:02,200
And at the proper moment, they stripped away these cloths, they pulled them off,

301
00:23:02,201 --> 00:23:07,200
and there in front of the astonished eyes of the British and the French code breakers

302
00:23:07,201 --> 00:23:10,740
were replicas of German Enigma machines.

303
00:23:11,680 --> 00:23:16,460
The British were able to smuggle out the machines two weeks before Hitler invaded Poland.

304
00:23:17,520 --> 00:23:20,560
They went straight to the government's code and cipher school,

305
00:23:21,340 --> 00:23:26,200
otherwise known as Bletchley Park, an ornate mansion 50 miles north of London.

306
00:23:27,400 --> 00:23:31,160
Alan Turing, a math genius recruited to Bletchley from Cambridge,

307
00:23:31,161 --> 00:23:35,200
soon made a fundamental insight into the workings of the bombs.

308
00:23:36,360 --> 00:23:42,720
The original Polish bomb would just keep sorting through German military messages

309
00:23:42,721 --> 00:23:44,360
until it came upon a key word.

310
00:23:44,840 --> 00:23:49,960
Alan Turing realized that there were so many possibilities in the German Enigma machine

311
00:23:49,961 --> 00:23:52,620
that they couldn't run through them all.

312
00:23:52,621 --> 00:23:56,500
And he had a different approach, and he devised a machine

313
00:23:56,501 --> 00:24:02,960
which would eliminate most of the possibilities, leaving relatively few to be tested.

314
00:24:04,400 --> 00:24:09,640
Each of Turing's bombs had 180 rotors that clicked round letter by letter,

315
00:24:10,280 --> 00:24:13,740
20 every second, until they hit the correct one.

316
00:24:15,160 --> 00:24:18,500
By this time, Bletchley was filled with hundreds of codebreakers

317
00:24:19,040 --> 00:24:24,280
who were divided into huts and labored in shifts to solve the flood of incoming messages.

318
00:24:24,281 --> 00:24:32,280
True meritocracy and rank didn't matter a hoot.

319
00:24:32,281 --> 00:24:40,400
And if the guy was good, regardless of what rank, he was given a free hand.

320
00:24:40,401 --> 00:24:45,820
It requires a certain almost contradictory combination of talents.

321
00:24:45,821 --> 00:24:59,360
On the one hand, this ability to deal with detail, but at the same time, this ability to achieve this almost sort of illogical leaps of insight and intuition.

322
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:05,140
It almost is that you wanted, you know, Beethoven with the soul of an accountant.

323
00:25:06,920 --> 00:25:11,260
Soon, Britain was deciphering large numbers of the German enigma communications.

324
00:25:11,261 --> 00:25:20,200
In 1943, a British engineer, Tommy Flowers, created Colossus, a mechanism that moved code

325
00:25:20,201 --> 00:25:26,900
breaking from electromechanical to purely electronic, which worked much faster.

326
00:25:26,901 --> 00:25:33,120
It read Germany's most complex coding system, the 12 rotor Lorenz machine, used by Germany's

327
00:25:33,121 --> 00:25:36,640
top commanders, including Hitler.

328
00:25:36,641 --> 00:25:42,860
Colossus was a proto-computer, capable of reading paper tape at 5,000 characters a second.

329
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:51,440
The Allied breaking of the German codes played a key role in such victories as the Battle of the Atlantic,

330
00:25:52,280 --> 00:25:54,620
the War in North Africa, and D-Day.

331
00:25:56,860 --> 00:26:01,060
Allied code breaking shortened the war, saved millions of lives,

332
00:26:01,520 --> 00:26:04,020
and highlighted the power of brains over bullets.

333
00:26:05,020 --> 00:26:07,240
It also launched the computer age.

334
00:26:08,700 --> 00:26:13,100
But there was another coding system used by the Japanese in World War II

335
00:26:13,101 --> 00:26:16,220
that the Americans were just as determined to crack.

336
00:26:17,100 --> 00:26:21,920
At the close of World War I, American cryptology was rudimentary.

337
00:26:22,860 --> 00:26:27,280
But since the early 1920s, a lone genius, William Friedman,

338
00:26:27,740 --> 00:26:31,900
had been developing statistical methods to solve the codes of the new rotor machines

339
00:26:31,901 --> 00:26:33,700
while heading the U.S. Signal Corps.

340
00:26:34,020 --> 00:26:45,500
When Japan switched to a mechanized coding system in the mid-1930s, Friedman's team of codebreakers was soon reading Japan's new red code for diplomatic communications.

341
00:26:46,960 --> 00:26:50,860
But solving Japan's codes was a torturous process.

342
00:26:51,420 --> 00:27:00,080
When you're trying to solve a code, you come in every day and look at an inscrutable bunch of letters or numbers.

343
00:27:00,680 --> 00:27:03,440
It's anguish. Maybe we should try this, maybe we should try that.

344
00:27:03,441 --> 00:27:07,500
day after day, month after month, year after year sometimes,

345
00:27:07,920 --> 00:27:12,040
until finally new messages come in which allow you to see

346
00:27:12,041 --> 00:27:14,700
some kind of pattern which you hadn't seen before.

347
00:27:15,440 --> 00:27:20,440
Then in February 1939, Japan introduced the Purple Machine,

348
00:27:21,160 --> 00:27:25,920
a mind-twisting coding system that employed several rotors and telephone switches.

349
00:27:27,360 --> 00:27:30,180
But Japan then made a critical human error,

350
00:27:30,181 --> 00:27:33,780
an important factor in code breaking throughout history.

351
00:27:35,040 --> 00:27:39,620
The Japanese helped out by sending the same messages in the red and purple systems.

352
00:27:40,480 --> 00:27:45,280
This allowed Americans to compare the two systems and make some breaks.

353
00:27:46,700 --> 00:27:51,560
A young MIT electrical engineer in Friedman's group, Leo Rosen,

354
00:27:52,360 --> 00:27:55,940
built a machine to simulate Purple's complex scrambling patterns.

355
00:27:55,941 --> 00:28:03,320
by 1940 the contraption had automated the decipherment of the Japanese purple

356
00:28:03,321 --> 00:28:05,820
code

357
00:28:05,821 --> 00:28:09,920
it was a great intellectual accomplishment made the decryption of

358
00:28:09,921 --> 00:28:13,960
these Japanese messages almost automatic it sometimes would allow the

359
00:28:13,961 --> 00:28:19,220
Americans to read the Japanese ambassadors mail before he did the

360
00:28:19,221 --> 00:28:22,820
purple code breakthrough led to one of the greatest intelligence crews of the

361
00:28:22,821 --> 00:28:23,521
war.

362
00:28:23,522 --> 00:28:30,560
Tokyo's ambassador to Berlin, Hiroshi Oshima, made a frontline tour of Germany's

363
00:28:30,561 --> 00:28:32,905
defenses in Normandy in 1943.

364
00:28:32,906 --> 00:28:37,920
He then transmitted a lengthy description back to Japan in the

365
00:28:37,921 --> 00:28:38,880
Purple Code.

366
00:28:38,881 --> 00:28:45,040
He describes in incredible detail, I mean, down to the caliber of the weapons

367
00:28:45,041 --> 00:28:51,580
in the German fortifications, the dimensions of the anti-tank ditches, where minefields

368
00:28:51,581 --> 00:28:53,240
It was really a gold mine.

369
00:28:54,020 --> 00:28:57,580
The solved message was soon on General Eisenhower's desk.

370
00:28:58,440 --> 00:29:03,080
He used it to plan the D-Day invasion of France that was launched six months later.

371
00:29:03,920 --> 00:29:10,280
Though purple had been cracked, America still worked desperately to solve the JN-25B code,

372
00:29:10,920 --> 00:29:12,760
Japan's secret naval communications.

373
00:29:13,840 --> 00:29:17,400
Joseph Rochefort, a longtime cryptanalyst fluent in Japanese,

374
00:29:18,100 --> 00:29:19,900
led the effort in Station Hypo,

375
00:29:19,901 --> 00:29:22,820
the Navy's code-breaking branch in Hawaii.

376
00:29:24,320 --> 00:29:28,480
By March 1942, after years of excruciating work,

377
00:29:29,060 --> 00:29:31,360
JN-25B was broken.

378
00:29:32,420 --> 00:29:35,520
On May 14th, a Japanese message was decoded

379
00:29:35,521 --> 00:29:39,100
outlining a huge invasion force heading to AF.

380
00:29:40,840 --> 00:29:43,900
Rochefort and Admiral Chester Nimitz immediately believed

381
00:29:43,901 --> 00:29:48,200
AF was Midway, one of America's key outposts in the Pacific.

382
00:29:49,200 --> 00:29:52,340
But Washington believed it was the Aleutian Islands.

383
00:29:53,040 --> 00:29:56,820
Really it was to shut up the doubters in Washington

384
00:29:56,821 --> 00:30:02,580
that Rochefort carried out what's now this famous stunt

385
00:30:02,581 --> 00:30:05,440
of having a message sent to Midway

386
00:30:05,441 --> 00:30:08,240
ordering them to send a message

387
00:30:08,241 --> 00:30:11,720
announcing that their desalination plant had broken.

388
00:30:11,721 --> 00:30:15,960
And then a few days later, a JN25 message was broken

389
00:30:15,961 --> 00:30:21,740
in which the Japanese said that they had intercepted this American message

390
00:30:21,741 --> 00:30:24,560
saying that AF was short of water.

391
00:30:24,820 --> 00:30:27,400
This really, you know, dotted the I and crossed the T.

392
00:30:28,620 --> 00:30:32,540
The invasion of Midway was the grand plan of Isoroku Yamamoto,

393
00:30:33,240 --> 00:30:35,060
Japan's legendary naval commander.

394
00:30:37,460 --> 00:30:39,600
The intent was to take over the island,

395
00:30:40,260 --> 00:30:42,780
draw out the remaining American fleet from Pearl Harbor,

396
00:30:43,040 --> 00:30:45,280
and destroy it with a surprise attack.

397
00:30:45,960 --> 00:30:55,320
On June 4th, 1942, the Japanese began to attack Midway, but lurking on the horizon with the coiled forces of the U.S.

398
00:30:55,321 --> 00:30:56,021
Navy.

399
00:30:57,060 --> 00:31:05,000
Knowing in advance what the Japanese plans were, allowed Nimitz to get his carriers there first and essentially to surprise the Japanese.

400
00:31:05,001 --> 00:31:18,233
We were able to hover off their flank and, unsuspecting of the Japanese, hurl ourselves on the Japanese, sink four carriers and send the Japanese battleships and

401
00:31:18,234 --> 00:31:24,480
We knew where the Japanese fleet was coming. carriers reeling back to Japan.

402
00:31:24,940 --> 00:31:32,380
That knowledge not only was the turning point for the Battle of Midway, but the Battle of Midway was the turning point in the entire Pacific War.

403
00:31:32,381 --> 00:31:36,940
It was a stunning and flawless operation.

404
00:31:38,140 --> 00:31:42,320
And in another part of the Pacific, the Americans were launching one of their own codes,

405
00:31:42,780 --> 00:31:45,000
one that would never be broken in World War II.

406
00:31:45,940 --> 00:31:49,000
Philip Johnston, a retired engineer living in Los Angeles,

407
00:31:49,640 --> 00:31:53,140
proposed using the Navajo language to the Marines in 1942.

408
00:31:55,040 --> 00:31:56,940
Johnston grew up on a Navajo reservation

409
00:31:56,941 --> 00:32:01,200
and was fluent in its complex forms of conjugation, sounds, and structure.

410
00:32:02,380 --> 00:32:06,700
It was remote. Only about 30 white Americans could speak it.

411
00:32:07,520 --> 00:32:10,300
No German, Japanese or Italian had ever studied it.

412
00:32:11,820 --> 00:32:17,760
The Navajo were also one of America's largest tribes with a potentially huge pool of code talkers.

413
00:32:17,761 --> 00:32:21,620
We used what we call a phonetic alphabet.

414
00:32:22,520 --> 00:32:24,620
So we use it by sound.

415
00:32:25,120 --> 00:32:31,760
It's natural sounds and guttural sounds and interchangeable.

416
00:32:32,380 --> 00:32:39,540
Within weeks of hearing Johnston's idea, the Marines were training 29 Navajo code talkers near San Diego.

417
00:32:40,660 --> 00:32:47,960
To avoid confusion, many Navajo words from the natural world were used to indicate specific military terms.

418
00:32:49,660 --> 00:32:53,400
Cheetelahi in Navajo is a turtle.

419
00:32:54,200 --> 00:33:02,240
And when you send a message and say cheetelahi on the radio, the receiver writes down tanks.

420
00:33:02,380 --> 00:33:04,580
T-A-N-K-S

421
00:33:04,581 --> 00:33:13,380
By August 1942, the first group of Navajo code talkers saw action during the invasion of Guadalcanal.

422
00:33:15,820 --> 00:33:21,900
By the end of the year, an additional 83 code talkers were serving in all six Marine Corps divisions.

423
00:33:22,380 --> 00:33:28,300
If you wanted to communicate a written message securely, you had to type it out or write it out.

424
00:33:28,301 --> 00:33:32,160
You had to give it to a code guy. This could take an hour or several hours.

425
00:33:32,380 --> 00:33:35,420
With the Navajo code, guys, it was instantaneous.

426
00:33:36,300 --> 00:33:38,980
The Navajo spoke directly over the radio,

427
00:33:39,740 --> 00:33:43,460
sending and receiving battlefield communications from both field headquarters

428
00:33:43,461 --> 00:33:46,360
and from the front lines in their native language.

429
00:33:47,200 --> 00:33:53,420
When you send the Navajo code, the receiver gets it, gives it to commanding officers.

430
00:33:53,580 --> 00:33:58,700
That's how fast it was, two minutes against two hours.

431
00:33:58,701 --> 00:34:03,700
By war's end, there were 420 Navajo code talkers.

432
00:34:03,701 --> 00:34:05,208
After the war, the U.S.

433
00:34:05,209 --> 00:34:11,700
government forbade them to talk of their unique contributions because of the secrecy of their code.

434
00:34:11,701 --> 00:34:16,700
Only in 1968 was the Navajo code made public.

435
00:34:16,701 --> 00:34:22,700
And in 1982, the code talkers were finally honored with a special commemoration day.

436
00:34:22,701 --> 00:34:29,700
America owes the Navajo code talkers the lives of many of its sons.

437
00:34:29,701 --> 00:34:34,700
The greatest tribute was that their code was never broken.

438
00:34:34,701 --> 00:34:39,700
But for code makers, the hunt for a truly unbreakable code is an obsession

439
00:34:39,701 --> 00:34:43,700
that continues into the age of computers.

440
00:34:43,701 --> 00:34:48,700
American code breaker William Friedman invented several cryptologic machines,

441
00:34:48,701 --> 00:34:52,020
Two of which were so secret, patents were never filed.

442
00:34:53,360 --> 00:34:55,540
Codes will return on modern marvels.

443
00:34:57,560 --> 00:35:01,420
For centuries, governments had controlled cryptology.

444
00:35:02,440 --> 00:35:04,920
That would change with the modern age.

445
00:35:05,940 --> 00:35:10,820
Soon after World War II, computers began to create codes of almost infinite complexity.

446
00:35:11,500 --> 00:35:13,600
There's a rule of thumb in cryptography.

447
00:35:13,601 --> 00:35:24,500
If you double the number of combinations that the code makes, you have to square the number of combinations that the code breaker has to try.

448
00:35:24,940 --> 00:35:30,620
So one guy is going from 5 to 10, but the code breaker has to go from 5 to 25.

449
00:35:31,300 --> 00:35:38,860
It's much easier to make the number of combinations virtually impossible to test.

450
00:35:38,861 --> 00:35:43,780
But this new encryption was still only accessible to the government.

451
00:35:44,880 --> 00:35:49,420
In 1952, President Harry Truman founded the National Security Agency,

452
00:35:50,060 --> 00:35:53,480
the greatest collection of computer and cryptologic talent in the world.

453
00:35:54,400 --> 00:35:58,720
Its charge was to intercept and decrypt intelligence from all over the globe.

454
00:35:59,980 --> 00:36:04,980
By the 1970s, more businesses began using computers to encrypt communications.

455
00:36:04,981 --> 00:36:11,720
But distributing secret keys needed to encode and decode messages remained cryptology's

456
00:36:11,721 --> 00:36:15,760
greatest historical problem.

457
00:36:15,761 --> 00:36:20,020
Businesses had to physically fly couriers to dozens of distant offices to deliver the

458
00:36:20,021 --> 00:36:27,380
secret keys to avoid interception and to ensure absolute security.

459
00:36:27,381 --> 00:36:31,420
But key distribution was not cryptology's only problem.

460
00:36:31,421 --> 00:36:36,960
By the 1970s, some people were concerned about the growing code-breaking power of the government

461
00:36:36,961 --> 00:36:40,700
because of computer technology.

462
00:36:40,701 --> 00:36:47,640
I think the biggest threat to privacy that we face is Moore's law.

463
00:36:47,641 --> 00:36:51,120
It's the power of computing doubling every 18 months.

464
00:36:51,121 --> 00:36:55,820
The human population is not doubling every 18 months, but the ability for computers to

465
00:36:55,821 --> 00:36:59,220
keep track of us is.

466
00:36:59,221 --> 00:37:06,820
technology with computers behind them to analyze the surveillance data can give rise to an omniscient

467
00:37:06,821 --> 00:37:15,260
government and it's not clear that democracy can survive omniscience by the mid-1970s a trio of

468
00:37:15,261 --> 00:37:22,100
Stanford cryptographers Whitfield Diffie Martin Hellman and Ralph Merkel created a theoretical

469
00:37:22,101 --> 00:37:27,420
model for coded computer communications that eliminated the need for a secret distribution

470
00:37:27,421 --> 00:37:28,121
of keys.

471
00:37:28,122 --> 00:37:33,480
It also sought to offer powerful digital encryption to a perceived future of

472
00:37:33,481 --> 00:37:38,453
mass computer users, as a counterweight to government spying.

473
00:37:38,454 --> 00:37:40,980
Called public key cryptography,

474
00:37:41,560 --> 00:37:48,120
it gave each computer user a pair of different but related keys, one public and one private.

475
00:37:50,340 --> 00:37:53,683
Now say Bob wants to send a secret message to Alice.

476
00:37:53,684 --> 00:37:55,740
Bob would use Alice's public key

477
00:37:55,741 --> 00:38:01,865
for encoding, but only Alice could use her private key to decode the message.

478
00:38:01,866 --> 00:38:02,740
No physical

479
00:38:02,741 --> 00:38:05,045
transportation of secret keys.

480
00:38:05,046 --> 00:38:09,500
And now even Bob and Alice could theoretically have access

481
00:38:09,501 --> 00:38:15,880
to encrypted communications hidden from the most powerful investigative forces in government.

482
00:38:15,881 --> 00:38:17,156
Complete privacy.

483
00:38:17,157 --> 00:38:22,560
None of the other developments in cryptography that I have seen from the

484
00:38:22,561 --> 00:38:28,320
The beginning of codes and ciphers themselves has had the impact or has been as original

485
00:38:28,321 --> 00:38:36,560
a concept in cryptography as Whitfield Diffie's invention of public key cryptography.

486
00:38:36,561 --> 00:38:44,180
Within a year, three MIT researchers, Ronald Rivest, Adi Shamir, and Leonard Adelman, created

487
00:38:44,181 --> 00:38:51,240
a series of mathematical equations using prime numbers that made Diffie's theory practical.

488
00:38:51,241 --> 00:38:56,160
As a side note, a group of three scientists, James Ellis, Malcolm Williamson and Clifford

489
00:38:56,161 --> 00:39:03,060
Cox, working for Britain's government communications headquarters, apparently independently invented

490
00:39:03,061 --> 00:39:10,800
public key cryptography by the mid-1970s, just before Whitfield Diffie.

491
00:39:10,801 --> 00:39:16,400
Their work was classified, and it wasn't until 1997 that public acknowledgement of their

492
00:39:16,401 --> 00:39:19,740
feat was recognized.

493
00:39:19,741 --> 00:39:26,600
As for the three MIT inventors, they formed RSA Data Security in 1982, the first company

494
00:39:26,601 --> 00:39:32,180
to commercialize public key cryptography and launch secure e-commerce.

495
00:39:32,181 --> 00:39:37,860
The market we were going after in 1982 was the Internet market.

496
00:39:37,861 --> 00:39:44,580
But we were so premature because of this to be 15 years later that the World Wide Web

497
00:39:44,581 --> 00:39:46,580
took off.

498
00:39:46,581 --> 00:39:51,500
But the National Security Agency did notice and desperately tried to stop the spread of

499
00:39:51,501 --> 00:39:55,060
public cryptography.

500
00:39:55,061 --> 00:40:00,980
In 1986, the government stopped Lotus Development from exporting its software because it included

501
00:40:00,981 --> 00:40:04,300
RSA encryption.

502
00:40:04,301 --> 00:40:09,620
Then in 1993, the government investigated Phil Zimmerman for creating and distributing

503
00:40:09,621 --> 00:40:16,380
Pretty Good Privacy, a software that used RSA encryption over the Internet.

504
00:40:16,381 --> 00:40:19,260
PGP could be used on anybody's personal computer,

505
00:40:19,760 --> 00:40:22,880
instead of just the giant machines used by the government and businesses.

506
00:40:24,360 --> 00:40:28,300
There was a time when NSA held a monopoly on this kind of technology.

507
00:40:28,860 --> 00:40:32,600
But over the past 20 years, that monopoly has eroded,

508
00:40:32,601 --> 00:40:38,020
and now academic cryptography has reached parity with the NSA.

509
00:40:39,120 --> 00:40:43,200
And PGP uses the best of the academic cryptographic algorithms

510
00:40:43,201 --> 00:40:45,260
and has made it available to the masses.

511
00:40:45,261 --> 00:40:52,820
The National Security Agency was terrified that criminals were now accessing the world's most powerful, unbreakable encryption.

512
00:40:53,900 --> 00:40:59,560
The NSA believed PGP was severely compromising its code-breaking and intelligence-gathering abilities.

513
00:41:00,080 --> 00:41:04,720
Many times I've been worried about if criminals and terrorists were to use this technology.

514
00:41:05,680 --> 00:41:07,640
And I did a lot of soul-searching on it.

515
00:41:07,880 --> 00:41:11,100
I sometimes lost sleep thinking about it.

516
00:41:11,101 --> 00:41:15,780
but individual governments like Stalin or Hitler

517
00:41:15,781 --> 00:41:20,240
have killed more people than all of the criminals of the 20th century combined

518
00:41:20,241 --> 00:41:21,460
all over the world.

519
00:41:22,160 --> 00:41:27,500
By 1994, RSA encryption was widely used in most U.S. software

520
00:41:27,501 --> 00:41:29,920
and companies were pushing to export it.

521
00:41:30,880 --> 00:41:35,040
The government urgently sought a compromise by introducing the Clipper chip,

522
00:41:35,560 --> 00:41:38,760
a government chip that would be installed in communications devices.

523
00:41:39,700 --> 00:41:44,480
The chip, like a wiretap, could only be accessed by the government through a court order.

524
00:41:45,720 --> 00:41:50,840
Law enforcement could still eavesdrop on suspected criminals, but grant the public a level of privacy.

525
00:41:51,340 --> 00:41:58,300
People were concerned that it was voluntary to start out with, but that it would become mandatory, that it would be pushed upon them.

526
00:41:59,060 --> 00:42:01,920
They were concerned about loss of personal privacy.

527
00:42:02,660 --> 00:42:08,520
Do you want to control your private communications just by yourself, or do you want to be partners with the government?

528
00:42:08,760 --> 00:42:11,500
in controlling your private communication.

529
00:42:12,340 --> 00:42:17,360
And ultimately, the society has decided that we want to keep privacy to ourselves.

530
00:42:18,560 --> 00:42:22,800
After a five-year fight and a huge public backlash to the proposal,

531
00:42:23,460 --> 00:42:27,820
the Clinton administration granted businesses the right to export public cryptography.

532
00:42:29,480 --> 00:42:36,040
By 1996, after MIT had published PGP in a book and posted it on its website,

533
00:42:36,041 --> 00:42:39,360
The government backed off its investigation of Zimmerman.

534
00:42:41,600 --> 00:42:46,840
Even though government has intruded increasingly into private lives

535
00:42:46,841 --> 00:42:52,100
and has greater capacities these days to intercept communications,

536
00:42:52,660 --> 00:42:58,460
the growth of encryption has made it possible for individuals to protect themselves

537
00:42:58,461 --> 00:43:03,500
and protect their privacy in a way that had never been able to be done before.

538
00:43:03,501 --> 00:43:10,040
You know, historically there's been a battle between cryptographers who make the codes and cryptanalysts who break the codes.

539
00:43:10,820 --> 00:43:18,140
And Edgar Allan Poe once said that any code that a man can invent, another man can break.

540
00:43:18,600 --> 00:43:21,200
But it turns out that that's probably incorrect.

541
00:43:21,660 --> 00:43:25,180
The best mathematical evidence we have is that that's not the case.

542
00:43:25,440 --> 00:43:29,160
That, in fact, code makers do win over code breakers.

543
00:43:29,400 --> 00:43:32,940
Code breaking will last as long as people make mistakes.

544
00:43:33,500 --> 00:43:37,820
but the code systems themselves are getting better and better.

545
00:43:38,260 --> 00:43:40,480
They can't be broken now if properly used.

546
00:43:41,200 --> 00:43:46,100
So I think we're in an era of the slow death of the code breaker,

547
00:43:46,540 --> 00:43:48,040
much as I regret it in a way,

548
00:43:48,840 --> 00:43:53,460
and increasingly in the era of the code maker.

549
00:43:54,380 --> 00:43:58,700
But throughout history, new codes have always been declared unbreakable,

550
00:43:59,200 --> 00:44:01,060
only to be eventually broken.

551
00:44:01,061 --> 00:44:08,060
As codes enter the realm of quantum computers, human ingenuity will be tested once again.

552
00:44:08,061 --> 00:44:11,060
Only time will tell the victors.

